plastic polluting our oceans harming  marine life and washing up in remote  corners of the world and today 60 years  of monitoring could unlock decades of  data for scientists although we knew  plastics were increasing getting a  consistent time series together to show  that increase has been very difficult  especially in the open ocean where we  don't have a lot of data and there's  been some microplastic studies that have  shown that increase but they haven't yet  been able to show the increase beyond  the 90s and that's where this study is  different it does show that increase in  the past the only way of monitoring  macro plastics like bottles or bags in  the sea would be by chance encounters  with marine life entangled in it but  researchers at the University of  Plymouth have been looking at data from  this machine which mimics the movement  of marine life it's been towed behind  ships collecting plankton since the  1930s but as its towed plastics have  come into contact with it which is  allowed scientists to look at 60 years  of data over 6.5 million  nautical miles and the data shown a  three-fold increase from 1% to 3% in the  amount of plastic that was captured in  the North Atlantic  just days ago the president of the  Seychelles made history with an  underwater message to the world it is  clear to me that it is under threat like  never before a call for help from a  country he says has been the victim of  global neglect  this research confirms what we suspected  the link between increasing plastic  production and plastic waste in the  ocean the report recommends sustainable  solutions to packaging and increasingly  firms are realizing there's money to be  made by finding an environmentally  friendly alternative companies have to  have a green policy today it's an  absolute essential for the future  survival and prosperity of any company  today most consumers today want to know  how the pack is disposed of and with  these adverse impact on the environment  the hope is this new research will be  able to unlock the scale of pollution  around the world a long-term dataset  that's been virtually non-existent until  now a solution though will take longer  Alley Fortescue Sky News
 

